Task 1: Run the IdM Configuration Tool
Subtask 1.1: Verify System Prerequisites
Before configuring IdM, ensure your system meets all requirements.

Check system hostname and FQDN:
hostname
hostname -f
Verify DNS resolution:
nslookup $(hostname -f)
dig $(hostname -f)
Check available memory and disk space:
free -h
df -h
Ensure firewall is configured:
systemctl status firewalld
firewall-cmd --list-all
Subtask 1.2: Prepare the IdM Installation
Set the system hostname if not already configured:
hostnamectl set-hostname idm.example.com
Update the hosts file:
echo "192.168.1.100 idm.example.com idm" >> /etc/hosts
Note: Replace 192.168.1.100 with your actual IP address

Verify time synchronization:
systemctl status chronyd
chrony sources -v
Subtask 1.3: Run the IdM Server Installation Command
Execute the IdM server installation with basic configuration:
ipa-server-install \
  --realm=EXAMPLE.COM \
  --domain=example.com \
  --ds-password=SecurePassword123! \
  --admin-password=AdminPassword123! \
  --hostname=idm.example.com \
  --ip-address=192.168.1.100 \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4 \
  --unattended
Parameter Explanation:

--realm: Kerberos realm name (typically uppercase domain)
--domain: DNS domain name
--ds-password: Directory Server password
--admin-password: IdM admin user password
--hostname: Fully qualified domain name
--ip-address: Server IP address
--setup-dns: Configure integrated DNS
--forwarder: DNS forwarders for external queries
--unattended: Non-interactive installation
Monitor the installation progress: The installation process will take 10-15 minutes. Watch for any error messages and note the final configuration summary.

Verify installation completion: Look for the message: "The ipa-server-install command was successful"

Task 2: Set Up Network and DNS Configurations
Subtask 2.1: Configure DNS Settings
Verify DNS zone creation:
ipa dnszone-find
Check DNS records:
ipa dnsrecord-find example.com
Test DNS resolution for IdM server:
dig @localhost idm.example.com
dig @localhost -x 192.168.1.100
Subtask 2.2: Configure Network Services
Ensure NetworkManager is properly configured:
systemctl status NetworkManager
nmcli connection show
Verify network interface configuration:
ip addr show
ip route show
Update DNS settings in NetworkManager:
nmcli connection modify "System eth0" ipv4.dns "127.0.0.1"
nmcli connection modify "System eth0" ipv4.dns-search "example.com"
nmcli connection up "System eth0"
Note: Replace "System eth0" with your actual connection name

Subtask 2.3: Configure Firewall Rules
Add IdM services to firewall:
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldap
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldaps
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=dns
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=ntp
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kerberos
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kpasswd
Reload firewall configuration:
firewall-cmd --reload
Verify firewall rules:
firewall-cmd --list-all
Task 3: Confirm IdM Service is Running
Subtask 3.1: Check Core IdM Services
Verify all IdM services are active:
systemctl status ipa
Check individual service status:
systemctl status dirsrv@EXAMPLE-COM.service
systemctl status krb5kdc.service
systemctl status kadmin.service
systemctl status named.service
systemctl status httpd.service
systemctl status ipa-custodia.service
systemctl status ipa-dnskeysyncd.service
View IdM service logs:
journalctl -u ipa -f
Subtask 3.2: Test IdM Functionality
Obtain Kerberos ticket for admin user:
kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted

Verify Kerberos ticket:
klist
Test IdM command-line tools:
ipa user-find admin
ipa config-show
Subtask 3.3: Access IdM Web Interface
Test web interface connectivity:
curl -k https://idm.example.com/ipa/ui/
Access the web interface:

Open a web browser
Navigate to: https://idm.example.com/ipa/ui/
Accept the self-signed certificate warning
Login with username: admin and the password you set during installation
Verify web interface functionality:

Navigate to Identity → Users
Navigate to Policy → Host-Based Access Control
Navigate to Authentication → Certificates
Subtask 3.4: Perform Basic Configuration Tests
Test DNS functionality:
dig @idm.example.com example.com SOA
dig @idm.example.com idm.example.com A
Test LDAP connectivity:
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://idm.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" -s base
Verify certificate authority:
ipa ca-find
ipa cert-find --all
Troubleshooting Common Issues
DNS Resolution Problems
If DNS queries fail:

# Check named service
systemctl status named
journalctl -u named -f

# Verify DNS configuration
ipa dnsconfig-show
Service Startup Issues
If services fail to start:

# Check service dependencies
systemctl list-dependencies ipa

# Review detailed logs
journalctl -xe
Web Interface Access Problems
If web interface is inaccessible:

# Check httpd service
systemctl status httpd
journalctl -u httpd -f

# Verify SSL certificates
openssl s_client -connect idm.example.com:443
Kerberos Authentication Issues
If kinit fails:

# Check KDC service
systemctl status krb5kdc

# Test with verbose output
kinit -V admin
